## Claim
Text: "Nigeria’s Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala has been appointed the new and first female Director-General of the World Trade Organisation (WTO)."
Claim date: October 08, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Is Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala a citizen of Nigeria?
Answer: Yes, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala is a citizen of Nigeria, as indicated by her roles as Nigeria's first female Finance Minister and Foreign Affairs Minister, and by her nomination as Nigeria's candidate for WTO Director-General.

Source URL: https://yashuaib.com/2020/07/wto-ngozi-okonjo-iweala/

### Was Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala appointed to the position of Director-General of the World Trade Organisation (WTO)?
Answer: Yes, based on the search result, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala was appointed to the position of Director-General of the World Trade Organisation (WTO), as the source states she "is the Director General of the World Trade Organisation (WTO)."

Source URL: https://live.worldbank.org/en/experts/n/ngozi-okonjo-iweala

### Which official body within the WTO is responsible for appointing the Director-General?
Answer: The official bodies responsible for appointing the WTO Director-General are the chairs of three major committees—the General Council (GC), the Dispute Settlement Body (DSB), and the Trade Policy Review Body (TPRB)—who form the "Troika" and lead the consensus-building process.

Source URL: https://www.csis.org/analysis/understanding-wto-director-general-selection-process

### Did the relevant WTO body formally approve Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala's appointment as Director-General?
Answer: The search result indicates that Dr. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala "officially assumes office" on March 1, 2021, which implies that formal approval had been granted, but it does not explicitly specify which WTO body formally approved her appointment or provide details of the approval process.

Source URL: https://caribbeantradelaw.com/tag/ngozi-okonjo-iweala/

### Has any woman previously served as Director-General of the World Trade Organisation?
Answer: No, based on the search result, there is no indication that any woman served as Director-General of the WTO before Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala, as it lists only male predecessors such as Supachai Panitchpakdi and Pascal Lamy.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna7840562

### Who were the other candidates, if any, for the WTO Director-General position in the same selection process as Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala?
Answer: Based on the WTO's official nomination list, the other candidates in the selection process alongside Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala were: Jesús Seade Kuri (Mexico), Amina Mohamed (Kenya), Yoo Myung-hee (Republic of Korea), Liam Fox (United Kingdom), Mohammad Maziad Al-Tuwaijri (Saudi Arabia), Tudor Ulianovschi (Moldova), and Abdel-Hamid Mamdouh (Egypt).

Source URL: https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/dg_e/dgsel20_e/dgsel20_e.htm

### What was the start date of Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala's term as WTO Director-General?
Answer: Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala's term as WTO Director-General started in March 2021, as she "assumed office in March 2021."

Source URL: https://www.foreignaffairs.com/authors/ngozi-okonjo-iweala

### What prior governmental or international organization roles did Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala hold before being appointed WTO Director-General?
Answer: Before her appointment as WTO Director-General, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala held several key governmental and international roles, including serving twice as Nigeria's Minister of Finance (2003–2006 and 2011–2015) and briefly as Minister of Foreign Affairs in 2006. Internationally, she was the Managing Director of the World Bank (2007–2011) and earlier served as a Vice-President and Corporate Secretary of the World Bank Group. As the source notes, she was "the former Chair of Gavi – the Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immunization" and also held positions as an African Union Special Envoy and a WHO Special Envoy.

Source URL: https://newclimateeconomy.net/about/global-commission/ngozi-okonjo-iweala

### Was Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala the consensus candidate for the WTO Director-General position?
Answer: Based on the search result, on October 8, 2020, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala was advanced as one of the two final candidates with the "broadest and deepest support from the membership," but the WTO's consensus-based selection process was not yet complete. The source states that a selection committee would next recommend the candidate most likely to gain consensus, meaning she was the leading candidate but had not yet been appointed by full consensus on that date.

Source URL: https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2020-10-08_IN11463_bd38a91fa174286c6e764409ec34dd52af2b0663.pdf

## Final Judgement
Based on the fact-check, the key findings are that while Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala was clearly a Nigerian candidate who subsequently became the WTO Director-General and is its first female leader, the evidence indicates that on the specific claim date of October 8, 2020, she had not yet been formally appointed. A source from that date shows she was a final candidate but that the consensus-based selection process was still ongoing. Multiple sources confirm she officially assumed the office in March 2021.

No critical information is missing to finalize the check.

The evidence clearly shows that as of October 8, 2020, the appointment process was not complete, and Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala did not assume the position until later. Therefore, the claim that she "has been appointed" on that date is inaccurate. The conclusion is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check confirms Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala is a Nigerian citizen and the first female Director-General of the WTO, but [evidence from October 8, 2020](https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2020-10-08_IN11463_bd38a91fa174286c6e764409ec34dd52af2b0663.pdf) shows she was then a final candidate in an ongoing selection process, not yet appointed. The appointment was later [formally confirmed](https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/dg_e/dgsel20_e/dgsel20_e.htm), and she [officially took office in March 2021](https://www.foreignaffairs.com/authors/ngozi-okonjo-iweala), making the claim on the specified date inaccurate.